Z||000008||BN Boomer||10-08-2001||04:05 AM||||You have to look at the flip side of the coin. The negative reputation is what keeps Fieros so inexpensive for us to enjoy. Most people have absolutely no idea how friendly the platform is to extensive modification and how well it can be made to perform.<P>I know older guys who say that American muscle cars from the 60s, he mentioned the AC Cobras specifically, were very unapreciated during most of the 70s and were pretty cheap to buy. Apparently the hot thing to have was a small foreign sports car, the Datsun Z cars seemed to be a hot ticket back then. I'm not saying the Fiero will ever gain the status of the Cobra as they are in entirely different leagues, but it just goes to show how things can fall in and out of favor over the years.||||reg||1
Z||000009||grinthock||10-08-2001||08:48 AM||||Boomer has it RIGHT ON!<P>HEY!!!  The next time someone says that say this<P>"As long as the world hates it?  I can enjoy 4 for the price of ONE of your imports"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||falconhulk||10-08-2001||09:38 AM||||BN Boomer, in case you did not notice this:  Fiero prices are going up up up.  A lot of people willing to pay way over book value.  There are still some good buys out there from people that are not familiar witht thier current popularity but they are getting less common.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Predator||10-08-2001||11:06 AM||||Some of the people who think the Fiero sucks are just uninformed. I was at Engineered Performance getting my car Dyno tuned, and this guy came over. I didn't get his name, but Ed the guy running the Dyno started talking to him. He started asking questions about my car, and the first thing out was. Didn't they have a problem catching fire? Ed and I just smiled at each other, and before I could say anything Ed explained that it was only the first year 4 cyls that could have had that problem, and then surprisingly to me started talking about all the potential the Fiero has for being a really nice street performance car. After that the guy changed his mind, and started wanting details from Ed. By then it was time for me to leave. The guy had a sweet Porsche turbo race car on the rack that Engineered was fixing and tuning for him. It just goes to show that some people may hear bad things about the Fiero, but have an open enough mind to change it. I think the guy actually got a little respect for the Fiero after Ed was done. It always makes me feel good when Ed starts talking about how nice he thinks my car is when he works on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. It means a lot coming from a guy who tunes high dollar performance and race cars for a living.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000012||pontiacs4me||10-08-2001||01:48 PM||||I usually dont get much crap from people about my car.  I actually get compliments about it, now and then, but its usually from girls.  Which doesnt bother me a bit.  I get funny looks when I pull in and theres 20 hot rods in the parking lot, but I dont care, because in 5 months Ill be able to keep up with any of them, if not even being able to humiliate most of them. I cant wait.<P>------------------<BR>Soon to be: L67 powered 87 coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BN Boomer||10-08-2001||01:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>BN Boomer, in case you did not notice this:  Fiero prices are going up up up.  A lot of people willing to pay way over book value.  There are still some good buys out there from people that are not familiar witht thier current popularity but they are getting less common.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are right. I have not noticed that extreme of a trend in general. What I have noticed are Fieros in decent shape more or less holding their value, or perhaps even appreciating mildly, but I don't believe I will live to see the day when Fieros selling for half a million dollars are common. I also notice Fieros advertised at reasonable and even excellent prices sitting for quite some time before being sold, mine included. I can't even get as much as a nibble or an insulting offer on a nice 85 GT that I am asking $2500 for. It's getting to the point where I would have to take less than what I have into the car just to move it. But rather than do that, I will just garage it and eventually do a fastback conversion and SC3800II swap.<P>Perhaps the market in the region in which you live is different you live. But even here on the forum, it seems like people only want to buy them for pennies on the dollar.<P><P>------------------<BR>Greg, aka Boomer<BR>Red 85 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.gmemd.com/locomotives/naf/images/sd70sm.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||falconhulk||10-08-2001||02:41 PM||||hey man cheap is cheap.  If you can get someone low enough do it.  Check out what decent cars go for on ebay.  under non recession times if i could find a nice 85 GT that did not have to be painted and had decent milage or was rebuilt I would jump on it for 2500.  By decent milage I mean under 60000.  In my area (MD, VA, DC) the only fieros I see advertised go for $5500 and up.  I am sure that some go for a lot cheaper that just are not advertised in the paper.  I paid $2500 for my 85 SE v6 but it only had 52000 miles on it.  I got it from a guy on ebay who sold me the car before ending the auction.  the final bid was $4500.  The thing is my car did not need anything dow to tires.  Its all original to my knowledge and well taken care of.  I am not saying that they are worth a million dollars.  Its still good when your car can sell for a few thousand over book value and its not classic yet.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
